WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.318 --> 00:00:19.179
Fantine died, the Thenardiers kept Cosette.

2
00:00:19.959 --> 00:00:24.041
It was not for love; it was because the girl was useful as a servant.

3
00:00:24.861 --> 00:00:26.742
The Thenardiers were dreadful people.

4
00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:33.338
At the sound of Madame Thenardier's voice, everything trembled: windows and furniture as well as people.

5
00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:37.200
She swore like a sailor and could crack a nut with her fist.

6
00:00:38.141 --> 00:00:42.984
She adored her daughters, Eponine and Azelma, but ignored her son, Gavroche.

7
00:00:44.005 --> 00:00:45.506
Monsieur Thenardier was no better.

8
00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:48.748
He pretended to have been a sergeant in Napoleon's army.

9
00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:54.292
He claimed to have risked his own life to save a wounded general at the Battle of Waterloo.

10
00:00:55.113 --> 00:00:59.416
He had even painted a flamboyant picture of his heroic deed on the sign in front of his

11
00:00:59.496 --> 00:01:05.938
inn. He dreamed of becoming rich, but at the moment he was fifteen hundred francs in debt.

12
00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:09.360
Young Cosette was the Thenardiers' only servant.

13
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.841
She washed, scrubbed, and swept.

14
00:01:12.461 --> 00:01:16.063
Although she was small and scrawny, she did all the heavy work.

15
00:01:17.503 --> 00:01:19.804
Madame Thenardier often beat Cosette.

16
00:01:20.384 --> 00:01:25.186
Monsieur Thenardier did not want to spend any money on Cosette, so she went without socks in the

17
00:01:25.226 --> 00:01:28.588
winter. The poor child was passive and silent.

18
00:01:29.228 --> 00:01:32.349
The Thenardiers' inn was like a trap in which she had been caught.

19
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.512
On Christmas night in 1823, Cosette was sitting in her usual spot under the kitchen table.

20
00:01:39.272 --> 00:01:45.115
Clothed in rags, she was knitting wool stockings for the Thenardier daughters, who could be heard laughing in

21
00:01:45.155 --> 00:01:45.795
the next room.

22
00:01:46.476 --> 00:01:48.797
Soon Madame Thenardier turned the faucet.

23
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:50.778
Cosette held her breath.

24
00:01:51.498 --> 00:01:57.336
Drinking water was scarce in Montfermeil, the town where Cosette lived with the Thenardiers.

25
00:01:57.356 --> 00:01:59.298
It was a 15-minute walk to the spring in the woods.

26
00:01:59.938 --> 00:02:05.801
During the day a hired man brought water to the Thenardiers' inn, but at night fetching water was

27
00:02:05.841 --> 00:02:06.722
Cosette's job.

28
00:02:07.602 --> 00:02:13.126
Four unexpected guests had arrived earlier in the evening, and Cosette was worried that the water would run

29
00:02:13.226 --> 00:02:14.106
out before morning.

30
00:02:15.827 --> 00:02:20.070
Madame Thenardier frowned as she watched a thin stream run into her glass.

31
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.372
"I guess there's enough," she finally announced.

32
00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:30.127
Cosette exhaled and resumed her knitting, but a long time passed before her heart stopped pounding.

33
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:36.529
Suddenly one of the guests came into the kitchen and said angrily, "You haven't watered my horse!"

34
00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:40.751
"Of course we have," said Madame Thenardier.

35
00:02:40.771 --> 00:02:45.813
"I know he hasn't had any water because he snorts when he needs a drink!"

36
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.394
Cosette came out from under the table.

37
00:02:48.414 --> 00:02:52.476
"Oh, monsieur, your horse had a big drink," she lied.

38
00:02:53.104 --> 00:02:56.267
"I brought him the bucket myself, and I talked to him."

39
00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:00.831
"Here's a girl the size of a mouse who can tell a lie as big as a house!"

40
00:03:01.191 --> 00:03:02.212
exclaimed the man.

41
00:03:02.232 --> 00:03:05.615
"Give my horse some water and say no more about it."

42
00:03:06.275 --> 00:03:08.097
Cosette slipped back underneath the table.

43
00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:12.961
"Cosette!" said Madame Thenardier.

44
00:03:12.981 --> 00:03:15.763
"Carry some water to this man's horse."

45
00:03:15.783 --> 00:03:21.128
"But there isn't any water," Cosette said quietly.

46
00:03:21.724 --> 00:03:23.386
"Then go fetch some!"

47
00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:27.209
Madame Thenardier's voice rattled every pot in the kitchen.

48
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.813
Cosette hung her head and fetched an empty bucket by the fireplace.

49
00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:36.036
The bucket was so large that she could have sat comfortably within it.

50
00:03:36.056 --> 00:03:38.238
"Here, Miss Toad."

51
00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:41.401
Madame Thenardier handed her a coin with the insult.

52
00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:44.303
"Get a loaf of bread at the bakery on your way home."

53
00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:47.466
Cosette put the coin in her apron and went out.

54
00:03:48.006 --> 00:03:53.162
There was a Christmas market in town, and a stall filled with toys stood across the road from

55
00:03:53.202 --> 00:03:53.563
the inn.

56
00:03:54.544 --> 00:04:00.789
In the place of honor sat a magnificent doll, nearly two feet tall and wearing a sparkling pink

57
00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:03.851
dress. Cosette stopped to admire it.

58
00:04:03.871 --> 00:04:05.893
"Oh, how beautiful!"

59
00:04:06.453 --> 00:04:07.013
She sighed.

60
00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:11.257
"That looks like the kind of doll a princess would own."

61
00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:13.719
"Stop your dawdling!"

62
00:04:14.219 --> 00:04:16.821
shouted Madame Thenardier from the inn's doorway.

63
00:04:17.622 --> 00:04:19.964
Cosette ran as fast as she could with her bucket.

64
00:04:20.384 --> 00:04:24.526
When she passed the last Christmas stall, she found herself in darkness.

65
00:04:25.226 --> 00:04:29.108
She kept on going and hurried by a woman walking in the other direction.

66
00:04:29.128 --> 00:04:34.130
"Who is that child and where in the world can she be going at this hour?"

67
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.330
The woman peered after Cosette.

68
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:39.212
"Oh, it's just the Lark."

69
00:04:40.152 --> 00:04:42.633
When Cosette came to the edge of town, she stopped.

70
00:04:43.113 --> 00:04:45.994
To go beyond the last Christmas stall had been difficult.

71
00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:49.736
To go farther than the last house seemed impossible.

72
00:04:50.284 --> 00:04:53.265
There was no light and Cosette was so afraid.

73
00:04:53.846 --> 00:04:56.927
She put the bucket on the ground and buried her face in her hands.

74
00:04:58.027 --> 00:05:02.909
When Cosette raised her head and looked around in the dark, she felt as if Madame Thenardier were

75
00:05:02.949 --> 00:05:03.710
there with her.

76
00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:08.231
Terrified, she ran into the woods until she reached the spring.

77
00:05:08.752 --> 00:05:14.174
She bent over the water and dipped in the bucket, never noticing the coin falling from her apron

78
00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:18.776
pocket. With great effort, Cosette set the bucket on the ground.

79
00:05:19.516 --> 00:05:21.917
It was so heavy that she could hardly lift it.

80
00:05:22.777 --> 00:05:25.419
She took a dozen steps and then stopped to rest.

81
00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:29.680
At this rate it would take her more than an hour to return to the inn.

82
00:05:30.481 --> 00:05:31.821
What a beating she would get!

83
00:05:32.521 --> 00:05:36.763
Suddenly a man appeared at her side and took the handle of the bucket from her.

84
00:05:37.343 --> 00:05:40.385
For some strange reason, Cosette was not afraid.

85
00:05:41.225 --> 00:05:44.546
This strong, white-haired man was Jean Valjean.

86
00:05:45.147 --> 00:05:48.168
He had not died when he fell from the mast of the Orion.

87
00:05:49.212 --> 00:05:53.375
In fact he had not fallen, but had jumped and hidden in a small boat.

88
00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:59.058
Eventually he had made his way to Paris, where he rented rooms in a remote part of the

89
00:05:59.098 --> 00:06:05.521
city. Earlier that day he had left Paris by stagecoach and arrived near Montfermeil.

90
00:06:06.322 --> 00:06:11.625
Valjean had disappeared into the woods, where he walked until he found a tree with a special mark

91
00:06:11.725 --> 00:06:11.965
on it.

92
00:06:12.826 --> 00:06:18.645
He had checked the ground nearby to be sure that it hadn't been disturbed, and then resumed walking

93
00:06:18.685 --> 00:06:19.305
through the woods.

94
00:06:20.285 --> 00:06:24.107
At that point he had met the little girl struggling with the bucket.

95
00:06:24.127 --> 00:06:25.988
"Are you going far?"

96
00:06:26.388 --> 00:06:27.008
he asked her.

97
00:06:27.028 --> 00:06:30.150
"About a 15-minute walk," she replied.

98
00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.671
"Why are you out here all alone?

99
00:06:33.371 --> 00:06:34.111
Where's your mother?"

100
00:06:34.131 --> 00:06:39.514
"I don't think I have a mother or if I did, I don't remember her.

101
00:06:40.274 --> 00:06:41.075
I live at the inn."

102
00:06:41.815 --> 00:06:43.035
"What's your name, little girl?"

103
00:06:44.788 --> 00:06:48.912
"Cosette." Valjean had to get control of his emotions before he could ask any more questions.

104
00:06:48.932 --> 00:06:51.834
"And who has sent you into the woods at night?"

105
00:06:53.295 --> 00:06:54.296
"Madame Thenardier.

106
00:06:54.777 --> 00:06:56.879
She's my mistress and she manages the inn."

107
00:06:56.899 --> 00:06:58.840
"Show me the way, Cosette.

108
00:06:59.401 --> 00:07:01.182
I'm going to stay at that inn tonight."

109
00:07:02.143 --> 00:07:05.486
Valjean walked quickly, but Cosette was able to keep up with him.

110
00:07:05.946 --> 00:07:11.831
By questioning the girl, he was able to learn that she worked all day while Eponine and Azelma

111
00:07:11.891 --> 00:07:16.137
played. "They have dolls and all kinds of things to amuse themselves with."

112
00:07:16.777 --> 00:07:17.557
Cosette sighed.

113
00:07:18.317 --> 00:07:23.299
"I don't have much time to play, but when I do, they won't let me touch their toys."

114
00:07:24.179 --> 00:07:27.820
They reached the town, where Cosette guided Valjean through the streets.

115
00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.521
She did not remember to stop at the bakery.

116
00:07:31.241 --> 00:07:34.782
As they drew near the inn, Cosette timidly patted his arm.

117
00:07:34.802 --> 00:07:37.903
"Please let me take the bucket now," she said.

118
00:07:37.923 --> 00:07:42.124
"If Madame Thenardier sees that someone carried it for me, she'll beat me!"
